      "snippet": "If the cost to society of an individual household’s choice to maintain a security system does not reflect a more accurate accounting of police response to false alarms, as well as the lost access to police services for others, then individuals who choose private systems will continue their substantial call on public resources. Conclusion Problems posed by alarm technologies, alongside the enormous growth of a private security industry , offer public police another opportunity to reeval-uate the services they now provide. In the case of home security systems, these inefficiencies have created an unex-pected form of inequality: home security devices have become a significant constituent of how police spend their time, and local public policies sup-37 THE EFFECTS OF PRIVATIZATION ON PUBLIC SERVICES port a significant allocation of resources by police to service these imperfect technological devices.",
